Jove wrote:
>
>   It looks like the real lessson from all this is that Healers shouldn't
> automatically max out their skills on any weapon.

And that they have the slots available for any really good artifact
weapon they get as a gift.  Going to Basic in Vorpy or especially
Frosty works fine.

>   Yes, I know knives don't get multi-shot.  So what?  As I said before,
> if you start throwing early enough the difference between multi- and
> single-shot isn't as great.
>
>   I'd say go with daggers.  d2 per fling at Skilled.  A neutral healer
> (Is there any other kind?) may get Magicbane as a divine gift.  +2 to
> hit when wielded or thrown.  (Knives only get +2 to hit when thrown.)
>
>   Which is not to say knives should be ignored.  Pick up any you find
> and throw them until you get some daggers.  Use autoquiver so you'll
> automatically throw knives after you run out of daggers.  Keep wielding
> the scalpel until you get to basic in dagger.  Then switch to wielded
> dagger to speed up #enhancing dagger skill.

The problem with knives is more one of early supply than damage
or skills.  Daggers come from dwarves, hobbits, elves, orcs, soldiers
including the watch.  Knives come from wights, soldiers including
the watch (equal chance with dagger) and some orcs.  In the early
game daggers accumulate much faster and sooner than knives.
Yet from a pure damage standpoint all the dagger categoies are at
least as good as knives.

So while I'd be happy to pick up and quiver all the knives that do
arrive in the game, the assorted classes of dagger are more
plentiful in the early game and seem to stay more plentiful as far
into the game as you play.

>   I'd say name your first non-cursed Elven dagger as Sting.  The orc
> spotting is a big help against orc hordes.

Yet more argument in favor of switching the wielded weapon to
dagger and enhancing dagger to Skilled earlier than knive.  Which
does not mean dropping all knives into your stash.  Then again
I like to carry a dozen or so permanent throwing weapons and I
will accumulate all types until I have that dozen, then drop the
weaker ones one by one as I get the better ones.  It rather looks
like the early abundant orcish daggers get kept in favor of knives.
So start by holding elven, regular, orcish daggers and knives,
then start dropping knives as the daggers get common, then start
dropping orcish daggers as the other two types get common, and
finally ending up with a dozen or so elven daggers.  That's my
dagger strategy with all classes just adding knives as the first
class to be dropped in favori of better.

> Then name Orcrist when
> possible to help chew up orc hordes maneuvered into a chokepoint based
> on Sting's orc spotting.

Elven broadsword as a non-artifact weapon is good enough to
use even being restricted in it, and they are plentiful.

> (You're getting the Staff.  What do you care about other artifacts?)

Since the Staff is two handed it would be *very* nice to get other
